    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No worries, I'm here to help, not to confuse you with goblin metaphors. I just like to use them to make complex concepts more relatable, like a goblin's love for shiny objects can make them more attracted to a particular investment strategy.\n\nNow, let's get back to the math. The interest is calculated using a formula called the \"interest rate times outstanding balance\" method. For example, if you have a loan with a balance of $10,000 and an interest rate of 6%, the interest for the month would be:\n\nInterest = Principal x Rate x Time\n\nIn this case, the interest would be:\n\nInterest = $10,000 x 0.06 x 1 (assuming a monthly payment)\n\nThis equals $600 in interest for the month. The remaining $400 would go towards the principal.\n\nHowever, as I mentioned earlier, the interest is calculated on the new balance, so the next month, the interest would be calculated on the new balance of $9,700, not $10,000. This is why the principal balance doesn't seem to decrease as much as you'd expect.\n\nThink of it like a never-ending tunnel, where the interest is the tunnel that keeps expanding, and",
